  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Unsettled weather will persist through next week. \nGreatest chance for rainfall will be over the mountains and \nadjacent foothills.\n\nSub-tropical high pressure remains parked over the western\nAtlantic  through this weekend, although it is gradually \nsquashed as the upper level trough axis over the central states \nshifts east. By next week, we will be further ensconced within \nthe mean trough as the main storm track builds south. This will\nincrease storm coverage and possibly a greater threat for more\norganized convection.\n\nThrough the weekend and all of next week, expect daily showers \nand thunderstorms. The main forcing mechanisms, especially \nthrough Monday, will be differential heating and interaction of \nthe southerly moisture fetch with the mountains and foothills. \nFor this weekend however, Saturday looks to have the highest \nchance for more widespread convection, with a surface trough in \nthe region. Overall though, with weak low level lapse rates and \nshear, storms should be disorganized and on the weaker side. \nMain concern will be flash flooding, and with slow storm \nmovement and very efficient rainfall rates, locations that see\nstorms persist for a longer duration could experience flash\nflooding.\n\nBetter storm coverage looks to arrive again by mid week as an\nupper trough begins to dip south. This will introduce slightly\nbetter shear/forcing into the region, therefore could see a\nlittle more widespread coverage and an uptick in severe\npotential. Although flash flooding from heavy rain still appears\nto be the main threat, some potential for damaging winds will\nexist as well.",
